---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Digr: Product, SEO & Growth Case Study | Mitch Chadban';
const description = 'How I built the product strategy, brand, technical SEO and organic growth foundation for Digr, a local-first music player for Mac.';
const schema = {'@context':'https://schema.org','@graph':[{'@type':'CreativeWork','@id':'https://mitchchadban.com/digr/#case-study','url':'https://mitchchadban.com/digr/','name':'Digr: Building a product and its organic growth engine from zero','headline':'Digr: Building a product and its organic growth engine from zero',description,author:{'@id':'https://mitchchadban.com/#person'},inLanguage:'en-AU'},{'@type':'BreadcrumbList',itemListElement:[{'@type':'ListItem',position:1,name:'Portfolio',item:'https://mitchchadban.com/portfolio-1/'},{'@type':'ListItem',position:2,name:'Digr',item:'https://mitchchadban.com/digr/'}]}]};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="https://mitchchadban.com/digr/" ogType="article" alternates={[{lang:'en-AU',href:'https://mitchchadban.com/digr/'},{lang:'es',href:'https://mitchchadban.com/es/portfolio/digr/'},{lang:'x-default',href:'https://mitchchadban.com/digr/'}]}>
  <Fragment slot="head"><script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} /></Fragment>
  <article class="digr-case-study"><div class="digr-case-study__inner">
    <header class="digr-case-study__head"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__meta">Product Strategy / SEO / Content / UX / Brand / GTM</div><h1>Digr: Building a product and its organic growth engine from zero</h1><p class="digr-case-study__intro">An independently developed local-first music player for people who still own their music — and an exercise in building product, positioning, brand and distribution as one connected system.</p></header>
    <div class="digr-case-study__system" aria-label="Digr project system overview"><div class="digr-case-study__system-top"><span>DIGR / 2026</span><span>LOCAL-FIRST AUDIO SOFTWARE</span></div><div class="digr-case-study__wordmark">digr<span>_</span></div><div class="digr-case-study__system-bottom"><span>PRODUCT</span><span>POSITIONING</span><span>CONTENT</span><span>LEARNING</span></div></div>
    <div class="digr-case-study__body"><main class="digr-case-study__main">
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">01 / The opportunity</p><h2>Music ownership still has a product problem</h2><p>Modern music software has made streaming the default mental model: search, subscribe, and let the service hold the library. That is a good fit for many listeners, but not for everyone. A substantial group still maintains collections made up of ripped CDs, downloads, lossless files and music accumulated over decades.</p><p>Those collections carry more than metadata. They hold taste, memory and years of careful organisation. The software around them often sits at one of two extremes: highly technical tools built for audiophile workflows, or capable desktop library applications whose visual language has not kept pace. Digr began by exploring the space between them.</p><p>The opportunity was not simply to make another audio player. It was to build a contemporary product around ownership, while treating product, brand and acquisition as related design problems from the start.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">02 / Defining the product</p><h2>Principles before the feature list</h2><p>Digr is a <a href="https://getdigr.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">local-first music player for Mac</a> built around the files and folders people already own. The strategy is deliberately narrower than “music software” and more useful than “a better streaming app”.</p><div class="digr-case-study__principles"><div><strong>Music ownership first</strong><span>The user’s own library is the foundation.</span></div><div><strong>Local by default</strong><span>The library does not need to be uploaded before it becomes useful.</span></div><div><strong>No account required</strong><span>Playback and management should not begin with a sign-up wall.</span></div><div><strong>Large libraries count</strong><span>Thousands of tracks are a first-class use case.</span></div><div><strong>Expressive, not nostalgic</strong><span>Character without reproducing old limitations.</span></div><div><strong>Useful interoperability</strong><span>Google Cast keeps local music from feeling trapped.</span></div></div></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">03 / Building the brand</p><h2>Early desktop music culture, reinterpreted</h2><p>The product evolved through earlier naming and concept exploration before Digr became the final name. The identity draws from early-2000s desktop music software, Y2K interfaces, physical audio hardware, record collecting and the pleasure of finding something unexpected in a library.</p><p>The aim was not literal nostalgia. It was to take the personality of Winamp-era software without bringing along its limitations. Tactile controls, expressive skins, display references, a dark interface and an Acid Lime accent give the system a point of view while keeping it credible in a premium 2026 software environment.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">04 / Product and engineering</p><h2>The positioning has to be true in the architecture</h2><p>Digr is being developed with Tauri 2, React, TypeScript and a Rust/Tauri backend, with SQLite and a local library architecture supporting the desktop experience. The product surface includes library scanning, folder watching, artwork management, playback, queues, media keys, scrubbing, volume, EQ, visualisers, skins, external-drive reconnection and casting.</p><p>If local-first is part of the promise, the application has to work locally rather than marketing a cloud product as privacy-friendly. Product language and technical architecture constrain each other in useful ways.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">05 / Designing search into launch</p><h2>Build relevance around the problem, not around a content quota</h2><p>SEO was considered before public launch. The early strategy builds topical relevance around local music players, Mac music players, large music libraries, FLAC and ALAC playback, library organisation, ripping and owning music, local versus cloud libraries, casting local music and privacy-conscious software.</p><p>The content architecture covers category education, problem-led help, comparison research and product-adjacent education. A useful article does not need to turn into an advertisement for Digr; it needs to help someone understand a decision they are already trying to make.</p></section>
      <section class="digr-case-study__rule"><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">06 / The product-truth rule</p><h2>Content cannot be more ambitious than the product</h2><p>Articles are not allowed to imply that Digr supports something it does not currently support. Sometimes the honest answer is to explain a capability that is planned, constrained or not implemented yet. That creates a better editorial standard and keeps product, content and brand aligned.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">07 / Technical SEO foundation</p><h2>Make the site easy to understand before asking it to perform</h2><p>The marketing site is built in Astro with static HTML and minimal hydration, hosted on Vercel. The foundation includes unique canonical URLs, titles and descriptions, Open Graph metadata, an XML sitemap, a separate image sitemap, RSS, structured data, an accessible heading hierarchy and internal-link validation.</p><p>Preview environments are deliberately noindex, nofollow. Structured data is kept honest too: the site does not fabricate <code>SoftwareApplication.offers</code> while the product is not purchasable.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">08 / Building conversion before launch</p><h2>Interest needs somewhere useful to go</h2><p>Search traffic before general availability is only useful if it creates a way to learn. The marketing site bridges that gap with a native branded waitlist form, a Brevo-backed list, clear success and error states, a completion event for analytics, privacy copy and a support contact.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">09 / Measurement setup</p><h2>The starting line is part of the case study</h2><p>Initial measurement uses Google Analytics 4, Google Search Console and Ahrefs. The questions are practical: which pages are discovered and indexed, which query clusters appear, what earns impressions and clicks, which pages become organic landing pages, and how often interest becomes a waitlist signup.</p><p>No results are being claimed yet. This case study captures the starting line.</p></section>
      <section><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">10 / What exists today</p><h2>An infrastructure for learning, before scale</h2><p>At the time of writing, Digr’s marketing site is live, the desktop application is under active development, the waitlist is open and the initial organic content architecture is in place. Search Console has begun its discovery and indexing phase.</p><p>This case study will be updated as meaningful acquisition and product data becomes available.</p></section>
      <section class="digr-case-study__next"><p class="digr-case-study__kicker">11 / What I’ll measure next</p><h2>Turn search behaviour into product learning</h2><ul><li>Which search clusters earn impressions first?</li><li>How does comparison content perform against informational content?</li><li>What is the organic-to-waitlist conversion rate?</li><li>Which product problems create the strongest demand?</li><li>Can search behaviour help prioritise future product development?</li></ul><p>Digr is an experiment in treating product, positioning, brand, content and distribution as one system from the beginning. The interesting result at this stage is that the infrastructure for learning is already present before launch.</p></section>
    </main><aside class="digr-case-study__side"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__card"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__label">Project</div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__value">Digr / Independent product</div></div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__card"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__label">Industry</div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__value">Consumer software / Music</div></div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__card"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__label">Market</div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__value">Global</div></div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__card"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__label">Stage</div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__value">Pre-launch / Early access</div></div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__card"><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__label">Role</div><div class="portfolio-project-ctm__value">Product / Brand / UX / SEO / Content / GTM</div></div></aside></div>
    <div class="portfolio-project-ctm__footer"><a class="portfolio-project-ctm__back" href="/portfolio-1/">Back to Portfolio</a></div><nav class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav" aria-label="Project navigation"><a class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-item" href="/kryoz/"><span class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-label">Previous Project</span><span class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-title">Kryoz Gardening</span></a><a class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-item" href="/pinos-restaurant/"><span class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-label">Next Project</span><span class="portfolio-project-ctm__nav-title">Pino’s Restaurant</span></a></nav>
  </div></article>
</BaseLayout>
